## Analysis of the four remaining gaps

**inputevents (0.819)** — the residual 26 % of placeholder rows use itemid pairs absent from the train slice. I benchmarked the fall-back candidates on exactly those train rows: `originalamount` **63/71 (88.7 %)** exact vs ingredient amount 6/71 (8.5 %) vs `totalamount` 2/71 vs rate×duration 0/71 — and it is right for **all 68** `FinishedRunning` orders. The chain (factor → originalamount → ingredient → totalamount → rate×duration) is therefore optimal; nothing left to gain.

**outputevents (0.608)** — charttime overwritten with the epoch. I benchmarked 8 estimators (storetime ∓15/30/45/60 min, rounded hour, raw storetime, +15 min), a per-itemid offset, and 4 interval-based rules (prev/next/midpoint/hour-boundaries/closest-to-storetime-minus-delay): **none beats `floor(storetime)`**. The file is not ordered by charttime (13/30 item groups monotone), so the charting delay is not reconstructible.

**chartevents (0.995)** — the validation slice has **6.3 %** overwritten charttimes vs 1.9 % in train; the neighbour-based restoration is already the best rule. I verified the valuenum is 99.975 % correct on aligned train rows and that **no placeholder valuenum remains** on either slice, and that all 547 aliased itemids resolve (no ambiguous suffixes).

**demographics (0.596)** — `anchor_age`=999 is unrecoverable: ages are masked in the discharge notes, there is no age itemid in the chart events, and the ECG/radiology texts carry no age (all searched). The sex of the 6 cohort subjects without any note is unknown; the anchor-year-group of subjects missing from `patients.csv` is a property of the deidentified timeline.

